# Ship: Hussla additions

Where this differs from `ship.mdc`, this wins for this project.

- **Shipping releases.** The merge to `main` runs `.github/workflows/release.yml`: it tags the
  next version, makes a draft GitHub release (notes from merged PRs), pushes the image
  (`ghcr.io/bretperry/hussla:<X.Y.Z>`, `X.Y`, `latest`) and attaches the binaries, and only then
  publishes the release as Latest. Nobody opens GitHub to tag.
- **Version bump.** Patch by default (newest `vX.Y.Z` + 1). For a bigger one, put
  `release:minor` or `release:major` in the **ship PR's title** before merging, as a word of its
  own; the merge commit's message carries the title, and the title is all the workflow reads. Use a merge commit (the default),
  so the title lands in the head commit's message.
- **After the merge, confirm the release:** the `Release` run on `main` is green and
  `https://github.com/bretperry/hussla/releases` shows the new version with its binaries. Name
  the version when you say it shipped.
- **A failed image or binaries job:** `gh run rerun <id> --failed` (`branching.mdc`); the release
  stays a draft until they pass. A full rerun resumes too: main's head already carries the tag, so
  it publishes that version again instead of cutting a new one, and does nothing once the release
  is published. Re-running an older ship's run after a newer one shipped publishes its version
  without taking Latest or the `latest` image; an older run that never tagged releases nothing.
- **Updating the NAS is still a separate step:** bump the pinned `image:` tag
  (`docs/install/nas.md` → Updating).
